{"compressedText":"# Field Guide: Preparing a Home for a 72-Hour Power Outage\n\nPrepare an ordinary household to get safely and comfortably through an outage of up to 72 hours. Assumes a temperate climate and no backup generator. Work the sections in order; earlier ones matter most.\n\n## Water\n- 4 litres per person per day = 12 litres per person for 72 hours; half for drinking, half for cooking and basic hygiene.\n- Food-grade containers, kept out of direct sunlight; replace stored water every 6 months.\n- If a storm is forecast, also fill the bathtub — bathtub water is for flushing toilets and washing, never drinking.\n\n## Food\n- A dedicated shelf of non-perishable food for 3 days: canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, shelf-stable milk.\n- Pick foods needing no cooking; own a manual can opener, because an electric one is useless.\n- Eat in order: perishable fridge food first, then the freezer, then the emergency shelf.\n\n## Refrigerator and freezer\n- Unopened fridge stays safe about 4 hours; a full freezer about 48 hours, but a half-full freezer only about 24 hours — so keep the freezer consolidated and full, even with containers of frozen water.\n- Keep both doors closed as much as possible; tape a reminder note on each door.\n- Discard any perishable food over 2 hours above 4 degrees Celsius; when in doubt, throw it out.\n\n## Light\n- Battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as primary light; avoid candles entirely, since they are a leading cause of house fires during outages.\n- At least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries for each light.\n- One headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen, placed to be found in the dark.\n\n## Heat and safety\n- Never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window: each produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal.\n- Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep.\n- In cold weather, gather everyone in one small south-facing room with its door closed; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater.\n- If the indoor temperature falls below 10 degrees Celsius and you cannot stay warm, move to a warming centre or a neighbour's home.\n\n## Communication and information\n- Battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts; know your local emergency station's frequency beforehand.\n- Whenever a storm is forecast, charge every phone and power bank — a full 20,000 mAh power bank recharges a typical phone about 4 times.\n- Put your phone in low-power mode at the start of the outage.\n- Agree in advance on one out-of-area contact whom every household member texts, because texts get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls.\n\n## Medical needs\n- A first-aid kit and a 7-day supply of every prescription medication; rotate them so they stay in date.\n- Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally stays usable at room temperature for up to 28 days, but confirm the rule for your specific medication with a pharmacist beforehand.\n- Write your pharmacy, doctor, and poison-control numbers on paper, because a dead phone is not a phone book.\n\n## When the power returns\n- Wait 5 minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise.\n- Check the freezer: food that still contains ice crystals can be safely refrozen.\n- Restock everything used within one week, starting with water and batteries.\n- Write a one-page note on what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one gap before the next outage.\n","expectedMetric":610,"hardConstraintNote":"Still a self-contained instruction document: it keeps the full title, all eight original section headings in order, the scope statement (72h, temperate climate, no generator, work in order), and every actionable instruction with its quantity, item list, ordered step, threshold, and reason. A reader with only this document can execute every original action; nothing points outward or assumes deleted context. Measured at 610 words by the frontier's counting rule (tr -s '[:space:]' '\\n' | grep -c .).","killedLeverCitations":["none — notes/KILLED_LEVERS.md has no filed K-ids as of 2026-07-09; this lever fences no prior negative result"],"lens":"restructurer","leverId":"restructure-to-section-bullets","rationale":"The original is narrative prose: each section is 2-4 sentences of connective tissue ('so it is worth', 'and make sure you', 'because a dead phone is not a phone book') wrapped around a fact core. My lens converts every section to a tight bullet list keyed to the same headings, collapsing the framing while keeping each fact — and each fact's reason — as its own clause. Structural moves: (1) preamble trimmed to three clauses; (2) every 'Store/Keep/Use/Install' lead verb dropped where the imperative is implied by the list; (3) the three freezer timings kept as one comparative clause rather than a pipe-table, because the counting rule tokenises '|' as separate words and a table would re-inflate the count; (4) sentence-joining semicolons replace 'and then'/'so that' padding. No fact is merged away: reasons that a comprehension probe could target (candles cause fires, CO is odourless and fatal, texts beat voice on congested networks, dead phone is not a phone book, grid must stabilise) are each retained verbatim in meaning. Net 730 -> 610 by the frontier's exact counting command (tr -s + grep -c), a ~16% structural reduction that leaves the full fact set intact rather than reaching toward the optimistic 400 ceiling at the cost of dropped facts.","title":"Convert each section's prose into imperative bullet lists and collapse the preamble"}